  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\nA ridge of high pressure will maintain dry weather into this\nevening. Much of the area should remain dry tonight, though a\nweak shortwave is progged to advance eastward tonight, crossing\nthe Lower Great Lakes region. This wave, and warm advection,\nshould produce scattered showers for area north of PIT\novernight.\n\nRidging is expected to strengthen again across the Upper Ohio\nValley region on Thursday, ahead of a trough approaching from\nthe Midwest. Dry and warm weather is expected, with highs around\n10 degrees above average. \n\nThe shortwave, and an associated surface cold front, is expected\nto approach and cross the region on Friday. The progression of\nthe front has increased some, resulting in a faster arrival of\ncloud cover and showers. This will further limit any possible\ndestabilization. Shear does increase with faster flow aloft,\nthough ML CAPE is progged to remain below 250 j/kg. This should\nlimit not only the severe weather potential, but the overall\nchances for thunder. Showers are expected though, with the area\nin the right entrance region to an upper jet. Generally expected\n1/4 to 1/2 inch of rain with the front, with NBM probabilities\nfor an inch of rain at 20 percent or less. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\nModel ensembles indicate broad upper troughing should persist\nacross the NE CONUS from the weekend into the middle of next\nweek. A southern stream shortwave could bring a few showers to\nareas south of PIT on Sunday, otherwise mainly dry and\nseasonably cool weather is expected through the period as\nsurface high pressure builds under the broad trough by early\nnext week.",
